I am with the unanimous three-storey stacking reading that has been consolidated in Rounds 1-3 and I think Round 4 can close the remaining citation nuance.

The four cola are not a top-to-bottom component “报菜名” parse. They are the parlour counting cadence for a symmetric three-level pile:

* 一横 一横 又一横 = the three storey/horizontal bars, i.e. the three levels of the pile;
* 一竖 一竖 又一竖 = the three left-hand uprights belonging to those three levels;
* 一竖在左 一竖在右 = the explicit left/right framing of a storey, the pair of uprights that closes each level;
* 一竖 一竖 又一竖 = the three right-hand uprights that close the three storeys.

That is the 3 H – 3 V left / left-right / 3 V right inventory. It is accounted for completely and with no leftover strokes, and it does so without importing any components that the wording never names.

品 pǐn is the modern abstracted normative form of that inventory: three horizontal bars held together on the left and on the right by vertical bars, the nine-stroke piling glyph. 晶 jīng and 森 sān are the historic poetic illustrations of the very same counting motif – three 日 and three 木 – and that is why the verbalisation is traditionally quoted with them.

田/丰/豊 fail on the pure 3-horizontal / twice-repeated 一竖一竖又一竖 with an explicit left/right framing clause, and 田 canonically has only two horizontals in the parlour wording. 帚/常 are component-decomposition overfits: 帚 would require a 彐–冖–巾 reading with 冖 never mentioned and the second vertical triad made redundant, and 常 brings in 口/小/尚 material that the riddle never introduces.

On the “one after another” stacking wording the cleanest modern canonical referent is therefore 品, with 晶/森 as its traditional poetic equivalents. I will keep my final vote in the 品 family and, for the current usage of the riddle, cite 品.

